<h2 id="skeletal" class="scroll-mt-24">Skeletal Procedures (the Foundation)</h2>
<p>These change the underlying bone — the foundation of the face. They give the most dramatic, lasting change because they alter the framework on which every soft-tissue feature sits. Once the skeleton is in the right position, skin, muscle, and fat drape over it more naturally.</p>
<div class="aspect-[16/9] w-full rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg border border-surface-container-high my-10">
<img alt="Skeletal facial surgery planning with imaging" class="w-full h-full object-cover" src="https://picsum.photos/1200/600?random=skeletal-facial-surgery"/></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Orthognathic surgery</strong> — moves the upper jaw (maxilla), lower jaw (mandible), or both into a balanced position. It corrects bite problems, improves facial proportions, opens the airway, and is the single most powerful procedure for changing the lower face. Recovery typically takes 2–4 weeks off work, with full <a href="recovery-time-after-jaw-surgery-complete-healing-timeline-and-care-tips.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">healing timeline explained here</a>. Read more on <a href="what-is-orthognathic-surgery-purpose-procedure-and-recovery-explained.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">what orthognathic surgery involves</a> and the <a href="jaw-surgery-orthognathic-complete-guide-for-patients.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">complete patient guide to jaw surgery</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Genioplasty</strong> — chin reshaping (advancement, reduction, vertical change). A small chin can be advanced, a long chin shortened, and asymmetry corrected. Often combined with orthognathic surgery for a balanced profile.</li>
<li><strong>Cheek augmentation</strong> — implants or bone reshaping. Used for flat or under-projected midface. Results are permanent and look natural when sized correctly.</li>
<li><strong>Rhinoplasty</strong> — nose reshaping. Changes the bridge, tip, nostrils, and angle between nose and lip. Functional benefits (better breathing) are often combined with cosmetic reshaping.</li>
<li><strong>Orbital (eye socket) surgery</strong> — for prominent eyes, sunken eyes, or asymmetry of the eye position. Often done for thyroid eye disease or congenital conditions.</li>
<li><strong>Mandibular angle implants</strong> — for a stronger, more defined jawline. Popular among patients with a rounded or weak lower face.</li>
</ul>
<p>For the appearance benefits of jaw surgery specifically, see <a href="can-jaw-surgery-improve-facial-appearance.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">can jaw surgery improve facial appearance?</a> Patients with sleep or breathing issues alongside facial concerns should also read <a href="can-jaw-surgery-help-with-sleep-apnea.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">can jaw surgery help with sleep apnea?</a></p>
<h2 id="soft" class="scroll-mt-24">Soft Tissue Procedures</h2>
<p>Soft tissue procedures work on skin, muscle, and fat rather than bone. They are excellent for refining what skeletal surgery has already positioned correctly, and for age-related changes like sagging, volume loss, and wrinkles. Recovery is usually shorter than for skeletal surgery, but results are not permanent.</p>
<div class="aspect-[16/9] w-full rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg border border-surface-container-high my-10">
<img alt="Soft tissue facial cosmetic procedures" class="w-full h-full object-cover" src="https://picsum.photos/1200/600?random=soft-tissue-facial"/></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Facelift</strong> — lifts and repositions the lower face, jowls, and neck. Modern techniques preserve volume rather than just pulling skin tight. Results last 8–12 years on average.</li>
<li><strong>Neck lift</strong> — tightens neck muscles (platysma) and removes loose skin under the chin. Often combined with facelift for a smoother jawline.</li>
<li><strong>Blepharoplasty</strong> — eyelid surgery for upper lids (droopy skin), lower lids (bags), or both. Patients often look 5–10 years younger and report better peripheral vision after upper lid surgery.</li>
<li><strong>Brow lift</strong> — lifts the forehead and brows. Useful for a heavy brow or tired look. Endoscopic techniques use small incisions and heal in 1–2 weeks.</li>
<li><strong>Lip lift / lip augmentation</strong> — shortens the upper lip or adds volume. A lip lift is permanent and ideal for an ageing long upper lip.</li>
<li><strong>Fat grafting</strong> — transfers fat from your own abdomen or thigh to the face. Used for cheeks, temples, and under-eye hollows. A portion of the grafted fat survives permanently.</li>
<li><strong>Scar revision</strong> — improves the appearance of existing scars from acne, injury, or previous surgery.</li>
<li><strong>Ear surgery (otoplasty)</strong> — pins back protruding ears or reshapes cartilage. Often done in teenage years, but suitable for adults too.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="nonsurgical" class="scroll-mt-24">Non-Surgical Options</h2>
<p>Non-surgical treatments are best for early signs of ageing, skin quality issues, and patients who want a test run before committing to surgery. They are not substitutes for skeletal or major soft-tissue procedures, but they complement them well. Most require maintenance every few months to a year.</p>
<div class="aspect-[16/9] w-full rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg border border-surface-container-high my-10">
<img alt="Non-surgical facial rejuvenation options" class="w-full h-full object-cover" src="https://picsum.photos/1200/600?random=non-surgical-facial"/></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Botulinum toxin (Botox)</strong> for wrinkles and masseter reduction. Softens forehead lines, crow's feet, and frown lines. Masseter injection slims a square jawline and can help with bruxism. Effects last 3–6 months.</li>
<li><strong>Dermal fillers</strong> for volume and contour. Hyaluronic acid fillers add volume to cheeks, lips, chin, and under-eyes. Last 6–18 months depending on product and area.</li>
<li><strong>Thread lifts</strong> (limited, temporary). Dissolvable threads lift sagging tissue for 1–2 years. Suitable for mild laxity in the right patient.</li>
<li><strong>Skin resurfacing</strong> — laser, chemical peels, and microneedling. Improve texture, tone, acne scars, and pigmentation. Usually a series of 3–6 sessions.</li>
<li><strong>Skin tightening</strong> — radiofrequency and ultrasound devices (Thermage, Ultherapy). Stimulate collagen for gradual tightening over 3–6 months.</li>
<li><strong>Hair restoration</strong> (PRP, transplant). PRP (platelet-rich plasma) supports thinning hair. FUE/FUT transplant moves healthy follicles to balding areas with permanent results.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="choose" class="scroll-mt-24">How to Choose</h2>
<p>The right procedure for you depends on what specifically bothers you, your underlying anatomy, your age, and your long-term goals. The right surgeon matters more than the right procedure — a good specialist will guide you to what actually serves your face rather than what is being marketed that month.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Identify what specifically bothers you</strong> — bring reference photos and write down what you want to change. Vague goals lead to vague results.</li>
<li><strong>Get a proper facial analysis from a qualified specialist</strong> — a maxillofacial surgeon evaluates skeletal proportions, soft tissue, and airway together, not just the symptom you noticed.</li>
<li><strong>Distinguish between <em>structural</em> issues and <em>soft-tissue</em> issues</strong> — a weak chin is structural (needs genioplasty), a thin lip is soft-tissue (responds to filler or lip lift). Treating the wrong layer is the most common reason patients feel their results did not deliver.</li>
<li><strong>Consider long-term results, not just immediate change</strong> — fillers and threads fade; skeletal surgery lasts a lifetime. Weigh cost over 10–20 years, not just the first invoice.</li>
<li><strong>Choose a surgeon, not a procedure</strong> — the right surgeon will recommend the right option. If a clinic pushes a procedure before examining you, that is a red flag.</li>
<li><strong>Ask about the recovery and the risks</strong> — review the <a href="side-effects-of-maxillofacial-surgery-risks-complications-and-prevention.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">risks and complications of maxillofacial surgery</a> and the <a href="recovery-time-after-jaw-surgery-complete-healing-timeline-and-care-tips.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">healing timeline</a> so your plan is realistic.</li>
</ol>
<p>For finding the right specialist, see <a href="how-to-choose-the-best-maxillofacial-surgeon-in-hyderabad.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">how to choose the best maxillofacial surgeon in Hyderabad</a> and <a href="difference-between-a-dentist-and-a-maxillofacial-surgeon.php" class="font-semibold text-secondary">dentist vs maxillofacial surgeon</a>.</p>
